WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:04.960
In this video,

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:09.280
we come back to the formula of the determinant of a matrix.

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:11.660
What will this be equal to?

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:16.200
What will be the formula if we have the first row as the reference?

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.780
The first element 'a11'

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:20.960
will be multiplied with something.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:24.160
Now this something is very important.

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:25.660
For the sign here,

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:27.560
we multiply it with 'minus 1'

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:30.740
raised to the row number plus the column number.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.760
As 'a11' lies in the first row and the first column,

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:38.460
we raise 'minus 1' to '1 plus 1'.

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:41.180
And to find out what this is multiplied with,

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.280
we exclude the row and the column of 'a11'

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:47.500
and write the determinant of what remains.

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:52.060
Determinant of this will equal 'a22 times a33'

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:56.260
minus 'a23 times a32'.

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:58.940
This will be the first part of the formula.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.800
Two more elements remain in the first row,

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:04.800
'a12' and 'a13'.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:07.380
What will 'a1'2 be multiplied with?

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.060
'Minus 1' raised to 1 plus 2,

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:13.440
multiplied by the determinants of these four elements.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:16.020
'a21' times 'a33'

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:19.980
minus 'a23 times a31'.

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:23.360
And what will 'a13' be multiplied with?

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:26.460
Pause the video and write the correct product here.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:33.660
This is what we will have.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:38.180
'Minus 1' raised to '1 plus 3' times this determinant.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:39.660
We knew this formula,

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:43.060
we just saw it in a bit more detailed fashion.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.140
Now there are a couple of terminologies

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:47.020
we need to be familiar with.

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:49.440
For the element 'a11'

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:51.900
this part here is called the minor.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:55.200
Yes, this is a minor of 'a11'

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.960
and this entire part is called the cofactor of 'a11'.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.620
Yes, this is the minor of 'a11'

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:05.800
and this is the cofactor of 'a11'.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:09.040
Similarly, this is the minor of 'a12',

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:12.400
and this is the cofactor of 'a12'.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:17.220
For 'a13' this is the minor and this is the cofactor.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:21.260
A good way to remember this is by knowing that minor means small,

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:23.080
the smaller part is minor

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:25.940
and the bigger part is the cofactor.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.540
Every element in the matrix has a minor and a cofactor.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:35.880
So let's zoom out and find the minor and cofactor of 'a32'.

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:38.520
The minor is the determinant of what's left

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:42.700
after excluding the row and column of that element.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:44.000
For 'a32',

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:47.580
we eliminate the third row and the second column.

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:51.140
The determinant of these four elements will equal

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:54.220
'a11' times 'a23'

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:58.280
minus 'a13' times 'a21'.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:01.380
This is the minor of 'a32'.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:02.840
For the cofactor

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:07.580
we just multiply this with 'minus 1' raised to '3 plus two'.

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:10.980
This will be the cofactor of 'a32'.

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:15.120
'Minus 1' raised to '3 plus 2' times this.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.940
So can we derive a relation between the cofactor

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:20.960
and minor of an element in a matrix?

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:22.180
Yes.

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:26.820
What will be the cofactor of 'Aij' where 'i' is the row number

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:29.120
and 'j' is column number?

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:34.060
Remember, this multiplied by the minor gives us the cofactor.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:38.060
So cofactor of 'Aij' will equal 'minus 1'

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:42.820
raised to 'i plus j' times the minor of 'Aij'.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:47.200
This is the relation between the minor and cofactor of an element.

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:49.760
The minor is the smaller determinant part.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:53.500
And multiplying it with this gives us the cofactor.

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:57.160
We will look at an example in the next part of the lesson.

